insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3149, $q$A Person, Not a Pretext$q$, $q$a-person-not-a-pretext$q$, $pt$When someone is harmed, concern can quickly become possession. People begin speaking for the injured person, negotiating around her, and using her pain to justify what they already wanted to do. The person at the center can disappear from her own story.

Dinah was the daughter of Jacob and Leah. After Jacob’s household settled near Shechem—an ancient city associated with Tell Balata beside modern Nablus in the West Bank—Dinah went to visit the women of the land. Shechem, son of the local ruler Hamor, took her and raped her (Genesis 34:1–2).

The chapter tells us what nearly every man wanted next. Shechem wanted Dinah as his wife. Hamor wanted a marriage agreement and wider economic ties. Jacob waited for his sons to return. Dinah’s brothers wanted revenge. The text does not record Dinah’s wishes or give her direct speech.

Hamor proposed intermarriage, shared land, and trade. Jacob’s sons answered deceitfully: the men of the city must be circumcised before the families could join. Hamor and Shechem presented the arrangement to their townsmen partly as an economic opportunity. While the men were recovering, Dinah’s brothers Simeon and Levi killed every male, removed Dinah from Shechem’s house, and joined their brothers in plundering the city (Genesis 34:6–29).

Their anger named a real outrage. Their revenge multiplied the victims. When Jacob protested that their violence endangered the whole household, they answered by asking whether their sister should have been treated like a prostitute (Genesis 34:30–31). The question defended Dinah’s honor, but it still did not let us hear Dinah herself.

This story requires care. Sexual violence is Shechem’s responsibility, not Dinah’s. Her visit did not cause his crime. Marriage negotiations could not erase it, and her brothers’ massacre did not restore what was taken from her.

We may repeat the pattern whenever another person’s wound becomes material for our argument. We can publicize details they did not choose to share, demand a response that satisfies us, or make our anger the main event. Even righteous concern can stop listening. Listening matters.

Dinah’s silence in the text should make us slower, not freer, to speak over people who have been harmed. Dignity begins by remembering that a person is more than what happened to her. Care asks what she needs, respects what she can choose, and refuses to turn her pain into anyone else’s pretext.$pt$, $q$2028-04-16T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 1, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
