insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3147, $q$The Warning We Do Not Want$q$, $q$the-warning-we-do-not-want$q$, $pt$Sometimes the obstacle in front of us is not blocking the right path. It is protecting us from the path we are determined to take. Ambition makes that distinction hard to see.

Balaam (BAY-luhm) was a diviner from Pethor, near the Euphrates. Balak, king of Moab, feared the Israelites camped on the plains east of the Jordan, across from Jericho. He sent messengers offering Balaam payment to curse them (Numbers 22:1–7). View the map for this story.

God told Balaam not to go with the first delegation. When more honored officials arrived with greater promises, Balaam asked again. God permitted him to travel but commanded him to speak only what he was told. On the road toward Moab, an angel stood in the way. Balaam could not see the danger, but his donkey could (Numbers 22:12–22).

Three times the animal tried to avoid the angel. Three times Balaam beat her. Then God enabled the donkey to speak, and the angel opened Balaam’s eyes. The man hired for unusual spiritual sight had been less perceptive than the animal carrying him. He admitted his sin and offered to turn back, but the angel repeated the earlier command: go, and say only what God gives you (Numbers 22:23–35).

Balaam later blessed Israel instead of cursing it, frustrating Balak again and again (Numbers 23–24). Yet Scripture’s final judgment of Balaam is not flattering. Numbers 31:16 connects him with the scheme that drew Israelites into unfaithfulness at Peor, and later biblical writers remember his love of gain and corrupting influence.

His story unsettles the idea that insight automatically changes character. Balaam heard God, spoke true words, and received a warning dramatic enough to be unforgettable. Still, knowledge did not uproot the desire that kept bending him toward reward.

Our warnings are usually less spectacular. A trusted coworker asks why we are rushing. A family member notices how money is shaping a decision. A failed plan creates a pause we immediately try to overcome. We may call these interruptions when they are invitations to look again. Humility makes room for that second look.

I can become so committed to an outcome that resistance only makes me push harder. Balaam’s donkey asks a better question: what if the thing slowing me down sees a danger I have trained myself not to notice? Discernment begins when I stop punishing the warning long enough to hear it.$pt$, $q$2027-12-12T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 2, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
