insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3113, $q$Courage Can Grow in Quiet Steps$q$, $q$courage-can-grow-in-quiet-steps$q$, $pt$We sometimes treat courage as a switch: either someone speaks boldly at once or fear has won. Most people are more complicated. A conviction can begin privately, struggle for words, and only later become visible in action.

Nicodemus (nih-ko-DEE-muhs) was a Pharisee, a teacher, and a member of the Jewish ruling council. He came to Jesus at night in Jerusalem. John does not tell us why he chose that hour, so we should not pretend to know. The darkness did give him privacy for questions that could affect his standing (John 3:1–2).

Nicodemus opened by acknowledging that Jesus’s signs pointed to God. Jesus answered by telling him that no one could see God’s kingdom without being born again—or born from above. Nicodemus did not understand. His training and position did not spare him from having to receive life as a gift rather than an achievement (John 3:3–10).

John lets that unfinished conversation remain unfinished. Nicodemus does not make a dramatic declaration before leaving. Instead, he appears again later when other leaders want Jesus arrested. He asks whether their law condemns a man without first hearing him. It is a limited defense, but it is public enough to draw ridicule from his peers (John 7:45–52).

Finally, after Jesus is executed, Nicodemus joins Joseph of Arimathea in caring for the body. He brings a large mixture of myrrh and aloes, and together they wrap Jesus for burial (John 19:38–42). At the moment when association with Jesus could offer no obvious advantage, Nicodemus makes his connection visible.

The three scenes do not give us a complete spiritual biography. They do show movement: private approach, cautious objection, costly service. Courage grew through choices that became harder to hide.

That matters when we are tempted to dismiss a small faithful step because it is not yet heroic. Asking an honest question can be a beginning. Refusing an unfair judgment can be another. Showing up when reputation offers a reason to disappear can make belief tangible. Faithfulness often becomes visible before we feel fully prepared to explain what has changed within us.

I do not want to use gradual growth as an excuse for permanent silence. But I also do not want shame to crush the early movement of courage. The next honest step may look small. It still needs to be taken where someone can see it.$pt$, $q$2027-11-28T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 4, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
