insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3101, $q$What Power Will Not Surrender$q$, $q$what-power-will-not-surrender$q$, $pt$Power can begin as responsibility and slowly become something we believe we must protect at any cost. Once that happens, people stop looking like people. They become threats, obstacles, or insurance against losing control.

Athaliah (ath-uh-LY-uh) was the mother of King Ahaziah of Judah and a member of the northern royal house of Ahab. After Ahaziah was killed, she moved to secure the throne in Jerusalem. Second Kings says she began destroying the royal family—the very children and grandchildren whose lives stood between her and power (2 Kings 11:1).

One child survived. Jehosheba, Ahaziah’s sister, rescued the infant Joash and hid him with his nurse in the temple. For six years Athaliah ruled while the rightful heir grew up only a short distance from the palace (2 Kings 11:2–3).

That hidden child exposes the weakness inside ruthless control. Athaliah could command a kingdom, but she could not guarantee that every rival was gone. Violence had made her feared, not secure. The more her rule depended on eliminating threats, the more any surviving life could undo it.

In the seventh year, the priest Jehoiada organized guards around Joash and publicly crowned him. Athaliah entered the temple court, saw the young king, tore her robes, and cried, “Treason! Treason!” She was taken from the temple and killed near the royal palace (2 Kings 11:4–16). The woman who seized power by violence finally encountered a community that would no longer sustain her claim.

Our stakes are usually smaller, but the pattern can still appear. A leader withholds information so no one else becomes essential. A parent controls every choice because independence feels like rejection. A teammate discredits a capable colleague before that person can become competition. Fear speaks the language of protection while it steadily reduces other people’s freedom. The same fear can hide beneath competence, certainty, and even a sincere concern for stability.

Athaliah’s story does not ask us to admire the counterviolence or to imagine that every conflict has a simple hero. It does show how power becomes destructive when keeping it matters more than the people it was meant to serve.

I can notice the moment responsibility turns into possession. If another person’s growth feels like my loss, the answer is not to make them smaller. It is to loosen my grip before fear decides what I am willing to destroy.$pt$, $q$2027-11-21T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 5, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
