insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3088, $q$Longing Can Distort What We See$q$, $q$longing-can-distort-what-we-see$q$, $pt$Comparison often begins with something genuinely painful. We are not pretending when we say another person has what we deeply want. The trouble begins when that person’s life becomes the measuring stick for our own.

Rachel entered the story near Harran in present-day southeastern Türkiye as a shepherd tending her father Laban’s flock. Jacob loved her and worked seven years to marry her. Then Laban substituted Rachel’s older sister, Leah, at the wedding and required Jacob to work another seven years for Rachel (Genesis 29:9–30).

The sisters were placed inside a family arrangement neither had designed. Rachel had Jacob’s love, while Leah bore children. Genesis does not flatten either woman’s pain. Leah knew she was less loved. Rachel watched her sister build the family she could not yet have. When Rachel saw that she remained childless, she became jealous and demanded children from Jacob (Genesis 30:1–2).

Her grief was real. Her conclusion was not. Leah was not the cause of Rachel’s infertility, and Rachel’s worth was not reduced by her sister’s pregnancies. Yet comparison turned a shared wound into a contest. The household absorbed the consequences as both sisters gave servants to Jacob and named children inside their struggle for standing (Genesis 30:3–24).

We still do this in quieter forms. A colleague’s promotion can make our own work feel invisible. A friend’s marriage, health, family, or opportunity can become evidence that life has passed us by. Soon we are not only grieving what is missing. We are resenting the person who reminds us of it.

Rachel eventually bore Joseph, and later Benjamin. She died giving birth to Benjamin while the family traveled toward Ephrath, identified in Genesis with Bethlehem (Genesis 35:16–20). The text does not offer a tidy ending to the sisters’ rivalry. It gives us something more honest: being loved did not erase Rachel’s longing, and receiving what she wanted did not make her life safe from sorrow.

I need that honesty when comparison starts narrowing my sight. Another person’s good is not a verdict on my value. Their joy does not explain my pain, and their life is not the proper target for it. Sometimes faithfulness means sitting beside the loss before deciding what that loss says about us.

We can name what we want without turning someone nearby into an opponent. Grief deserves truth, care, and patience. It does not need an enemy.$pt$, $q$2027-11-14T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 6, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
