insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3073, $q$Power Cannot Define Her Whole Story$q$, $q$power-cannot-define-her-whole-story$q$, replace($pt$Power can force a moment into someone’s life and then make that moment the only story people remember. Naming the harm matters. So does refusing to let the person who caused it control the rest of the story.

Bathsheba (bath-SHEE-buh) was the wife of Uriah, a soldier in King David’s army. She lived in Jerusalem, the hill city that remains Israel’s capital today. One evening David saw her bathing, learned that she was married, and sent messengers to get her. The text gives David the royal power, the orders, and the responsibility (2 Samuel 11:1–5). View the map for this story.

When Bathsheba became pregnant, David tried to hide what he had done. He finally arranged for Uriah to be abandoned in battle and killed. After Bathsheba mourned her husband, David brought her into his house. The prophet Nathan confronted David and named his actions as evil (2 Samuel 11:6–27[[semicolon]] 12:1–10).

We should not soften that imbalance by turning the account into a romance. David was the king. Bathsheba was summoned. Scripture does not record her consent, private thoughts, or ability to refuse.

The child born from that encounter died. Later Bathsheba gave birth to Solomon, whom David loved and named as his successor (2 Samuel 12:15–25[[semicolon]] 1 Kings 1:28–35).

Years passed, and Bathsheba appeared again when another son of David, Adonijah, tried to take the throne. The prophet Nathan warned her that she and Solomon were in danger. Bathsheba went to the aging king, reminded him of his promise, and asked directly why Adonijah was acting as king. David responded by publicly confirming Solomon (1 Kings 1:11–40).

Bathsheba is no longer only the woman acted upon in 2 Samuel 11. In 1 Kings 1 she speaks inside a dangerous succession crisis and helps secure her son’s future.

That does not erase the earlier harm. Growth is not proof that the harm was useful, necessary, or secretly good. It means another person’s wrongdoing did not receive the final word about her agency.

I need that distinction when I listen to someone’s story. I can believe what happened, name the person who held power, and avoid demanding a tidy recovery. I can also make room for the person in front of me to be more than the worst thing someone else did.

Dignity begins there: not with rewriting the past, but with refusing to imprison a life inside it.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), $q$2027-09-26T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 7, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
