insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3063, $q$Bring the Person You Notice$q$, $q$bring-the-person-you-notice$q$, $pt$Influence is easy to confuse with visibility. We notice the person speaking to the room, leading the meeting, or receiving the credit. But some of the most useful work begins when someone notices one person and makes an introduction.

Andrew (AN-droo) was a fisherman from Bethsaida, near the northern shore of the Sea of Galilee in today’s Israel. He was Simon Peter’s brother and one of Jesus’s first disciples (John 1:40–44). View the map for this story.

John’s Gospel first shows Andrew listening to John the Baptist, who pointed to Jesus. Andrew followed Jesus, spent part of the day with him, and then went to find his brother. “We have found the Messiah,” he told Simon. Then Andrew brought him to Jesus (John 1:35–42, NIV).

Peter would later become the better-known brother. He preached publicly, led the apostles, and appears across the Gospels and Acts. Andrew’s first recorded contribution was quieter: he connected Peter with the person who would change both their lives.

The pattern appears again when a large crowd needed food. Philip calculated that even months of wages would not buy enough. Andrew noticed a boy carrying five small barley loaves and two small fish. He admitted that the food seemed far too small, but he brought the information to Jesus anyway (John 6:5–9).

Jesus fed the crowd. The miracle was not Andrew’s achievement, and the boy was not a prop. Andrew simply paid attention to a person and a possibility that others had not named.

Later, some Greek visitors wanted to see Jesus. They approached Philip, who went to Andrew. Together they told Jesus (John 12:20–22). Again, Andrew stood between a request and an introduction.

None of those scenes makes Andrew the center. That is what makes them useful. He did not have to control the outcome to help create the meeting.

I can practice that kind of attention in ordinary life. A new colleague may need the name of someone who can help. A quiet person may have the missing fact. A friend may be ready for a conversation but unsure how to begin.

Not every introduction will become a turning point. People are not projects, and help should not become pressure. Still, I can notice, ask permission, and open a door.

Andrew reminds me that influence is not always measured by how many people know my name. Sometimes it is measured by whether I saw the person standing beside me and helped that person take one honest next step.$pt$, $q$2027-09-19T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 8, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
