insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (3065, $q$Learn It Before You Teach It$q$, $q$learn-it-before-you-teach-it$q$, $pt$Advice becomes dangerous when the speaker has not done the work behind it. A polished explanation can hide shallow study. A public standard can hide private exceptions. The gap may stay invisible until other people begin building their lives on our words.

Ezra (EZ-ruh) was a Jewish priest and scribe living under Persian rule. His family belonged to the people whose parents and grandparents had been taken from Judah to Babylon. In the seventh year of King Artaxerxes, Ezra received permission to lead a group from Babylon to Jerusalem, a journey the book places across about four months (Ezra 7:1–9). View the map for this story.

Jerusalem was being rebuilt after exile, but buildings were only part of the repair. The returning community also needed to understand the law that shaped its worship and shared life.

Ezra 7:10 gives the order of his work: he “had devoted himself to the study and observance of the Law of the Lord, and to teaching its decrees and laws in Israel” (NIV).

Study came first. Ezra did not treat inherited words as decoration. He gave attention to what they said.

Practice came next. Knowledge was not enough. The teacher stood under the same instruction he would explain to other people.

Then came teaching. Ezra’s authority was connected to learning and obedience, not merely to his title or confidence.

That sequence does not make a leader perfect. Ezra’s later response to intermarriage in the community is difficult and should not be turned into a quick modern formula. The account involves covenant identity, vulnerable families, and decisions whose human cost is recorded but not fully explored (Ezra 9–10). Careful readers should resist using it to support prejudice or to make complicated families into symbols.

Even so, Ezra 7:10 offers a clear test for anyone whose work depends on words. Have I studied enough to know what I am claiming? Am I willing to live under the standard I recommend? Can I teach without pretending that my interpretation settles every question?

The pattern applies beyond a pulpit or classroom. A manager explains a policy. A parent sets a boundary. A friend offers counsel. In each case, credibility grows when words have passed through honest attention and personal practice.

I want to slow the order down. Learn before speaking. Practice before prescribing. Teach with enough humility to keep learning.

That kind of leadership may sound less impressive. It is also far safer to trust.$pt$, $q$2027-09-12T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 9, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
