insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2974, $q$Seen After Being Sent Away$q$, $q$seen-after-being-sent-away$q$, $pt$Being ignored can make a person feel smaller than the problem around them. Someone else makes the decision, holds the money, or controls the door. The person with the least power is left carrying the largest cost.

Hagar (HAY-gahr) was an Egyptian slave in the household of Abram and Sarai, later called Abraham and Sarah. Sarai had no children, so she gave Hagar to Abram in hopes of building a family through her. Hagar became pregnant, conflict followed, and Sarai mistreated her. Hagar ran away (Genesis 16:1–6). View the map for this story.

An angel of the Lord found her beside a spring on the road to Shur. Hagar explained that she was fleeing. The angel told her to return, but also promised that her descendants would be too many to count. Her son would be named Ishmael, meaning “God hears,” because God had heard her misery (Genesis 16:7–12).

Then Hagar gave God a name: “You are the God who sees me” (Genesis 16:13, NIV). The nearby well became known as Beer Lahai Roi, the well of the Living One who sees me. Its exact location is uncertain, somewhere in the wilderness south of Canaan between Kadesh and Bered.

Years later, Sarah demanded that Hagar and Ishmael be sent away. Abraham gave them food and water, and Hagar wandered in the Desert of Beersheba. When the water was gone, she placed Ishmael under a bush and moved away because she could not bear to watch him die (Genesis 21:8–16).

God heard the boy crying. The angel called Hagar by name, repeated the promise about Ishmael, and God opened her eyes to a well. She filled the water skin and gave her son a drink. Ishmael grew up in the desert, and Hagar found him a wife from Egypt (Genesis 21:17–21).

Being seen did not make the harm unreal. Hagar was still enslaved, mistreated, and displaced by decisions made above her. But those decisions did not define her worth or erase her future.

That matters whenever leadership turns people into tools. A worker absorbs an impossible schedule. A caregiver is left out of the family decision. Someone with less status is expected to stay quiet because speaking would be costly.

I cannot fix every wilderness, but I can ask who is carrying the cost of my choice. I can listen before deciding, share power where I can, and refuse to treat another person as expendable.

Hagar’s story leaves us with a better question than whether overlooked people matter. The question is whether we will learn to see them before a crisis forces us to look.$pt$, $q$2027-08-15T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 12, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
