insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2962, $q$Loyalty Cannot Excuse Everything$q$, $q$loyalty-cannot-excuse-everything$q$, replace($pt$Loyalty can become a flattering name for helping someone avoid consequences. We tell ourselves we are protecting the leader, the family, or the team. Over time, solving every threat with secrecy or harm can make our “help” more dangerous than the original problem.

Joab (JOH-ab) was King David’s nephew and commander of Israel’s army. He was capable, courageous, and central to David’s rise. He was also willing to use violence when others chose restraint. View the map for this story.

Early in David’s reign, Joab’s brother Asahel died while pursuing Abner, commander of a rival force. Abner had warned Asahel to turn aside. Later, after Abner made peace with David, Joab drew him aside and killed him (2 Samuel 2:18–23[[semicolon]] 3:20–27).

David publicly mourned Abner and said Joab’s violence had happened without his approval. Yet Joab remained commander (2 Samuel 3:28–39).

That unresolved pattern continued. When David arranged Uriah’s death, Joab carried out the order by placing him in deadly fighting and withdrawing support (2 Samuel 11:14–17). When David later ordered the army to be gentle with his rebellious son Absalom, Joab killed Absalom after finding him helpless and caught in a tree (2 Samuel 18:5, 9–15).

Joab could also tell David truths others avoided. He confronted the king after Absalom’s death when David’s public grief humiliated the soldiers who had risked their lives for him (2 Samuel 19:1–8).

That complexity matters. Joab was not merely disloyal or loyal, wise or violent. His service contained all of it. The Bible lets us see skill and usefulness without asking us to call every method good.

Leaders help create this danger when they keep rewarding results while postponing accountability for how those results were achieved. David benefited from Joab’s decisiveness, distanced himself from Joab’s bloodshed, and still left the reckoning for his son Solomon (1 Kings 2:5–6).

I need to ask what my loyalty is protecting. Is it protecting a person from unfair attack, or protecting that person from the truth? Does my help preserve a mission, or excuse methods that betray it?

Real loyalty can say no. It can refuse an order, name a boundary, and tell a friend that usefulness does not cancel harm.

Joab solved many problems for David. The violence inside those solutions became a problem neither man could leave behind. Skill and courage matter, but character must govern both. Otherwise, today’s indispensable fixer can become tomorrow’s deepest liability.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), $q$2027-06-20T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 14, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
