insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2960, $q$What We Hide Still Spreads$q$, $q$what-we-hide-still-spreads$q$, $pt$A secret can feel contained because no one else knows it. But hidden choices still change budgets, trust, safety, and the decisions other people make. The secrecy may be private. The cost rarely is.

Achan (AY-kan) was an Israelite from the tribe of Judah during Joshua’s campaign in Canaan. Before Jericho fell, Israel was told that the city’s silver, gold, bronze, and iron belonged in God’s treasury. Other devoted things were not to be taken (Joshua 6:17–19). View the map for this story.

After the victory, Israel attacked the smaller city of Ai. The force was defeated, and about thirty-six men died. Joshua grieved and asked why God had brought the people across the Jordan only for this to happen (Joshua 7:2–9).

God’s answer connected the defeat to what had been hidden after Jericho. Israel had taken devoted things, stolen, lied, and placed the goods among personal possessions (Joshua 7:10–12).

The search narrowed by tribe, clan, family, and household until Achan was identified. Joshua asked him to tell the truth. Achan confessed that he had seen a beautiful robe from Babylonia, silver, and gold. He coveted them, took them, and hid them in the ground beneath his tent (Joshua 7:16–21).

Messengers found the items exactly where he said they were.

The chapter ends in severe judgment on Achan and his household (Joshua 7:22–26). The passage raises difficult questions about collective responsibility and punishment. We should not flatten those questions into a quick slogan or use this story to justify punishing families for one person’s wrongdoing.

The narrower warning is still clear: Achan’s hidden act changed what happened to other people. Soldiers entered battle without knowing the community was carrying a breach they had not addressed.

Modern secrets can work that way. A falsified number shapes someone else’s plan. Hidden harassment changes who feels safe. Money quietly redirected leaves another person explaining why the work cannot be completed.

Accountability should not become suspicion of everyone. It should create honest ways to surface problems early: clear records, shared oversight, safe reporting, and leaders willing to hear bad news.

When I am tempted to call a compromise private, I need to ask who could eventually carry its cost. Confession cannot undo every consequence, but bringing truth into the open is where repair can begin.

What we bury still occupies ground. What we hide still spreads through families, teams, congregations, and communities that trusted us.$pt$, $q$2027-06-13T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 15, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2958, $q$When People Become Pieces$q$, $q$when-people-become-pieces$q$, replace($pt$Power becomes dangerous when it stops seeing people as people. A relationship turns into leverage. A marriage becomes a message. Someone’s pain is treated as acceptable because it helps another person win.

Michal (MIH-kal) was a daughter of King Saul and the first wife of David. First Samuel says she loved David. Saul saw that love as an opportunity to place David in danger, setting a risky bride-price in the hope that David would die fighting the Philistines (1 Samuel 18:20–25). View the map for this story.

The plan failed, and Michal married David. Later, when Saul sent men to kill him, she warned David, lowered him through a window, and used a household idol and bedding to delay the pursuers. Her action saved his life (1 Samuel 19:11–17).

Then Michal’s own choices narrowed. Saul gave her to another man, Palti. Years later, after Saul’s death, David demanded her return as part of negotiations over the kingdom. Palti followed her, weeping, until he was ordered to go home (1 Samuel 25:44[[semicolon]] 2 Samuel 3:13–16).

The text reports these transfers without telling us what Michal wanted at either point. That silence should make us careful. We can describe what powerful men did without inventing her private response.

Later, Michal watched from a window as David danced before the ark entering Jerusalem. She despised him in her heart and confronted him when he returned. David defended his celebration sharply. Their exchange ends with the statement that Michal had no children (2 Samuel 6:16–23).

Readers debate how to judge that final conflict. Michal’s contempt is plainly present. So is a life repeatedly shaped by her father’s fear, David’s rise, and royal claims made around her.

Her story makes me ask how often a person’s difficult response is treated as the whole story while the history of pressure behind it disappears.

That does not excuse contempt or harmful speech. It does challenge me to hold accountability and context together. In a family, workplace, or church, people should never become symbols of loyalty or tools for settling someone else’s contest.

I can resist that by asking who has a voice, who bears the cost, and whether a decision protects the dignity of the people most affected.

Michal was not merely Saul’s daughter, David’s wife, or a figure at a window. She was a person inside those relationships. Good leadership remembers that before making its next move.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), $q$2027-06-06T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 16, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2956, $q$Repentance Has a Receipt$q$, $q$repentance-has-a-receipt$q$, $pt$An apology can describe regret without changing what the other person lost. Repair asks a harder question: what can I return, replace, correct, or make right? Zacchaeus did not leave that question vague. View the map for this story.

Zacchaeus (za-KEE-uhs) lived in Jericho, near the Jordan Valley in today’s West Bank. Luke calls him a chief tax collector and a wealthy man. Tax collectors worked inside Rome’s system and were widely distrusted because the work could reward overcollection (Luke 19:1–2).

When Jesus passed through Jericho, Zacchaeus wanted to see him. He was short and could not see over the crowd, so he ran ahead and climbed a sycamore-fig tree (Luke 19:3–4).

Jesus stopped, looked up, called him by name, and said he must stay at Zacchaeus’s house. Zacchaeus came down and welcomed him gladly. The crowd complained that Jesus had gone to be the guest of a sinner (Luke 19:5–7).

Then Zacchaeus stood and made his response measurable. He said he would give half his possessions to the poor. If he had cheated anyone, he would repay four times the amount (Luke 19:8).

The wording “if” does not require us to decide how many people he had cheated. What matters is that he named a process for repair. The people affected would not have to settle for a speech about his intentions.

Jesus answered, “Today salvation has come to this house,” and described his mission as seeking and saving the lost (Luke 19:9–10, NIV).

I notice the order. Jesus offered Zacchaeus welcome before Zacchaeus announced restitution. Grace did not pretend harm was unimportant. It gave him a place from which to face it.

That pattern can guide an ordinary apology. First, name what I did without shrinking it. Then listen for the cost to the other person. Correct the record, return the money, replace the damaged item, or change the process that allowed the harm. Some losses cannot be repaid, and forgiveness cannot be purchased. Still, concrete repair can show that regret has moved beyond words.

Zacchaeus also made his change public enough to be accountable. He attached proportions and consequences to it.

I may not need a dramatic promise, but I do need a next step someone else could recognize. Repentance becomes credible when it reaches beyond my feelings to the people who carried the cost.

A changed heart is not a receipt. Repair is where the numbers begin to show.$pt$, $q$2027-05-30T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 17, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2824, $q$Do the Necessary Next Thing$q$, $q$do-the-necessary-next-thing$q$, $pt$Pressure can leave us waiting for certainty that will not arrive in time. A child is in danger. A serious mistake comes to light. Someone must call for help, admit what happened, or take the next safe step. Courage is not always a grand plan. Sometimes it is doing the necessary thing in front of us.

Zipporah (ZIP-er-uh) was the wife of Moses. She first appears after Moses fled Egypt and settled with her family in Midian. Ancient Midian is usually placed east of the Gulf of Aqaba, in parts of today’s northwestern Saudi Arabia and southern Jordan, though its exact boundaries remain uncertain. The map page shows that wider region rather than claiming an exact home.

Zipporah’s father, Reuel, welcomed Moses after he helped the family’s daughters at a well. Reuel later gave Zipporah to Moses in marriage, and she gave birth to a son named Gershom (Exodus 2:15–22).

Years later, Moses started back toward Egypt with his wife and sons. God had called him to confront Pharaoh and lead the Israelites out of slavery. During the journey, the family stopped for the night. Then the story becomes startling: “the Lord met Moses and was about to kill him” (Exodus 4:24, NIV).

The passage is only three verses long, and it leaves important questions unanswered. It does not explain why the required circumcision had been delayed or what every gesture and phrase meant. We should not pretend the text gives us certainty it does not give.

What it does show is Zipporah acting. She took a flint knife, circumcised her son, touched Moses’ feet, and spoke of a “bridegroom of blood.” Then the danger passed (Exodus 4:25–26). Whatever else the difficult scene means, her decisive action stands at its center.

Later, Exodus says Moses had sent Zipporah and their two sons away. It does not say when or why. Her father brought them back to Moses after Israel left Egypt, and the family was reunited (Exodus 18:1–6).

Acting under pressure is not the same as acting without care. Panic grabs at anything. Courage looks for the next responsible step. It calls the doctor, tells the truth, moves someone away from danger, or asks a wiser person for help.

We may still have questions afterward. Zipporah’s story certainly leaves us with some. But we do not need every question answered before we protect what has been placed in our care. When the moment is urgent, faithfulness may begin with the next necessary thing.$pt$, $q$2027-05-23T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 18, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2954, $q$Integrity When Power Fails$q$, $q$integrity-when-power-fails$q$, $pt$Integrity does not guarantee protection. Sometimes the person who keeps faith is harmed by someone with more power. That is not a reason to abandon integrity. It is a reason to build leadership that never treats another person’s character as something to exploit.

Uriah the Hittite (you-RAI-uh) was a soldier in King David’s army and the husband of Bathsheba. His label “the Hittite” marks him as having foreign ancestry or family identity, yet he served among David’s elite warriors (2 Samuel 23:39). View the map for this story.

While Uriah was away with the army besieging Rabbah—modern Amman, Jordan—David saw Bathsheba in Jerusalem and slept with her. When she became pregnant, David summoned Uriah home from the battle (2 Samuel 11:1–6).

David told Uriah to go home. The plan would make the child appear to be Uriah’s. But Uriah slept at the palace entrance with the king’s servants instead.

When David asked why, Uriah pointed to the ark, Israel’s troops, and his commander Joab camping in the open. He would not enjoy home while they remained in the field (2 Samuel 11:7–11).

David tried again, even getting Uriah drunk. Uriah still did not go home (2 Samuel 11:12–13).

Then David sent him back to the army carrying a sealed message for Joab. The message ordered Joab to place Uriah where the fighting was fiercest and withdraw support so he would be killed. Joab obeyed, and Uriah died (2 Samuel 11:14–17).

The story gives Uriah no awareness of the plot. We should not invent noble speeches or private thoughts for him. His observable choices are enough. He acted in solidarity with people still facing danger, while his king used that loyalty as part of a cover-up.

This is why integrity and accountability belong together. Organizations cannot simply praise loyal people. They must create ways to protect them from leaders who may misuse trust, secrecy, or obedience.

If I lead, I need people who can question me and systems I cannot quietly bend. If I serve, integrity may also require raising a concern, preserving evidence, or asking for help outside the chain of command.

Nathan later told David the truth, and David admitted, “I have sinned against the Lord” (2 Samuel 12:13, NIV). Accountability finally reached the king—but not before Uriah paid the cost.

Integrity is precious. Good power protects it instead of turning it into a weapon.$pt$, $q$2027-05-16T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 19, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
