insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2835, $q$Orders Do Not Remove Responsibility$q$, $q$orders-do-not-remove-responsibility$q$, $pt$An order can make a wrong choice feel smaller. Someone with more power decided what should happen, so we tell ourselves the responsibility belongs to that person. But authority does not carry the whole moral weight of what our hands do.

Doeg the Edomite (DOH-g) served Saul, Israel’s first king, as his chief shepherd. He appears at Nob, a town of priests probably near Jerusalem, though its exact site remains uncertain. The map page shows the proposed area north of the city without claiming a confirmed location.

David had fled from Saul. At Nob, he asked the priest Ahimelek for food and a weapon. David claimed he was on the king’s business, so Ahimelek gave him consecrated bread and Goliath’s sword. Doeg was there and saw the exchange (1 Samuel 21:1–9).

Later, Saul accused his officials of hiding David’s actions. Doeg spoke up. He reported that Ahimelek had helped David. Saul summoned the priest and his family. Ahimelek explained that he knew nothing about David’s flight and had treated him as a trusted member of the king’s household (1 Samuel 22:6–15).

Saul ordered his guards to kill the priests. They refused. Then Saul gave the order to Doeg.

Doeg obeyed. He killed eighty-five priests and then attacked Nob, including its families and animals. One priest, Abiathar, escaped and reached David (1 Samuel 22:16–21). The story does not soften Saul’s command, and it does not treat Doeg as a tool without a choice. Other servants heard the same king and would not take part.

That difference matters. We may never face an order so terrible, but smaller versions appear at work and at home. A supervisor asks us to hide a safety concern. A leader wants an unfair report changed. A friend pressures us to pass along something meant to hurt another person. “I was told to” explains the pressure. It does not erase our part.

Responsibility may begin with a pause. What harm will this cause? What truth is being left out? Can I refuse, ask for the order in writing, or seek help from someone with the courage to challenge it?

Power can make obedience costly to resist. Doeg’s story does not deny that pressure. It shows why conscience matters most when an authority would like to borrow ours. Someone else may give the order, but I still have to answer for the choice I make.$pt$, $q$2027-05-09T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 20, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
